--- a/docs/internals/timing.md
+++ b/docs/internals/timing.md
@@ -169,6 +169,30 @@ aligned longword use the AGA fetch latch and do not request or wait for the
 same value twice. This read/write asymmetry is covered by
 `aga_68020_chip_reads_wait_for_data_but_writes_are_posted`.
 
+### Classic CPU/Copper transfer boundaries
+
+On the synchronous classic chip-bus path, a granted word transfers before
+the CPU's trailing colour clock. Chip-RAM stores publish each word at that
+boundary, including the two separate halves of a longword. Copper samples
+COPEN before the tail; an odd CPU grant can therefore expose an earlier
+even Copper restart phase. Other DMACON consumers retain their calibrated
+write phase. CPU COPJMP reads and writes take effect at the granted word's end,
+then enter the same two restart phases as a Copper-issued strobe. A pointer
+write while DMA is disabled preserves a pending restart and its live reload.
+The first restart phase is free; the second reserves a DMA slot for pointer
+reload without inventing an instruction-word memory transfer.
+
+A stock CPU chip/RGA cycle also waits when it would enter the Copper's
+pre-refresh turnaround. The guard considers the upcoming Copper request,
+not just the previous bus owner. CPU-started normal blitter StartDelay can
+overlap Copper, but Init and normal bus-free body phases require a free RGA
+clock and cannot enter that turnaround. Memory-transfer cycles and internal
+terminal phases retain their separate rules. The completion predictor walks
+the same phase-specific reservation. These distinctions are constrained by repeated
+A500 CPU/Copper instruction-word races, settled-WAIT controls and blitter
+source/readback races; they are not uniform timing offsets. The 020+ posted
+write path retains its separate timing.
+
 ### Deferred timed-device ticks
 
 The chipset and beam advance every colour clock, but the *timed devices*
@@ -431,9 +455,9 @@ wait for the blitter to go idle.
 
 ### COPJMP and frame reload
 
-- A CPU write to COPJMP1/COPJMP2 loads the Copper program counter
-  immediately, but the target list has no visible effect until the Copper
-  gets DMA slots to fetch it.
+- A CPU write to COPJMP1/COPJMP2 strobes at the granted word's end, then
+  enters the two Copper restart phases. The second phase reserves a DMA
+  slot and loads the live list pointer; instruction fetching follows it.
 - A CPU *read* of COPJMP1/COPJMP2 fires the same strobe: the register
   decode acts on the address alone (reading a write-only register performs
   a bus write of the floating data-bus residue into it -- the UAE/vAmiga
@@ -447,9 +471,9 @@ wait for the blitter to go idle.
   `timing-test/copprobe-jmpread.asm` (vAmiga-verified).
 - A Copper MOVE can update COP1LC/COP2LC; a later COPJMP strobe branches
   through the *current* value. A Copper MOVE to COPJMP1/COPJMP2 spends its
-  second word fetch on the strobe, then two more bus-free Copper cycles
+  second word fetch on the strobe, then two more Copper cycles
   (vAmiga COP_JMP1/COP_JMP2); the program counter reloads on the second of
-  those, so the first fetch from the new list lands three Copper cycles
+  those, reserving that slot, so the first fetch from the new list lands three Copper cycles
   after the strobe (verified against the vAmiga copper trace: a COPJMP2
   MOVE at hpos $04 fetches the target list's first MOVE at $0A, its write
   landing at $0C).
